1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,760
Why do moving a camera and changing its zoom
give different views of the same world?

2
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:10,220
First, real split-screen gameplay will help
separate cameras from output windows,

3
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:12,040
followed by a pixel-aspect calculation.

4
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:14,800
Then we connect field of view with zoom
numerically

5
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:17,480
and compare that with moving the camera
forward.

6
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:20,640
Finally, orthographic projection and a
split-screen

7
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:24,060
exercise will show which parameters to change.

8
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:29,000
Split Fiction divides the two players at a
central boundary.

9
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:32,120
Follow each character in its own view.

10
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:38,400
Different directions produce different
backgrounds and occlusion;

11
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:41,720
these are more than identical pieces of one
picture.

12
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:50,980
The camera determines the view; the output
window determines where that result goes.

13
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:53,680
We will calculate with our own defined diagram

14
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:57,080
rather than infer this game’s internal
settings.

15
00:00:57,700 --> 00:00:59,900
A render target stores rendering results,

16
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:03,680
either in a display buffer or a texture used
by another calculation.

17
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:06,920
Our output rectangle uses a top-left origin
with pixel

18
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:10,960
coordinates increasing rightward and downward.

19
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:14,020
Store the rectangle’s position and width and
height separately.

20
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:17,640
Split a1920-by1080 image vertically.

21
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:21,820
The left window begins at(0,0) with size960
by1080;

22
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:25,280
the right begins at(960,0) with the same size.

23
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:28,300
The left window’s last integer column is959;

24
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:31,600
position plus width gives the next boundary
at960.

25
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:34,100
One camera result may appear in two windows,

26
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:39,200
or different camera results may occupy them.
Storage and viewing are separate choices.

27
00:01:39,380 --> 00:01:43,100
Shadow maps and in-world monitors can store
results in textures.

28
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:46,480
Implementation and the full pixel
transformation come in the next lecture.

29
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:54,400
A1920-by1080 image has aspect16:9; an
individual pixel is not16:9.

30
00:01:54,660 --> 00:01:56,740
If display and resolution both have
aspect16:9,

31
00:01:56,740 --> 00:02:01,640
a physical pixel has equal width and
height:1:1.

32
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:05,460
Multiply physical display width-to-height by
resolution height-to-width;

33
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:07,420
all aspect ratios here use width divided by
height.

34
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:13,480
A4:3 pixel array stretched across a16:9
physical area gives physical pixel aspect4/3.

35
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:16,420
A circle drawn assuming square pixels becomes
horizontally stretched.

36
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:20,180
Fixing that with camera rotation or object
scale breaks other relationships.

37
00:02:20,420 --> 00:02:24,000
Moving or splitting a window does not change
physical pixel aspect,

38
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:25,920
although the rectangle’s aspect changes.

39
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:29,320
Off-screen bitmaps commonly assume square
pixels;

40
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:32,920
an anamorphic plan must record the later
display stretch.

41
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:37,660
The source’s1153-by720 is not
exactly16:10;1152-by720 is.

42
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,620
Verify ratios rather than memorize resolution
lists.

43
00:02:42,530 --> 00:02:46,110
Townscaper moves from a small waterside
building to a closer view.

44
00:02:46,110 --> 00:02:52,150
Choose a roof corner and window as landmarks.

45
00:02:53,803 --> 00:02:58,043
Orbiting reveals sides and stairs.

46
00:02:58,483 --> 00:03:03,957
A changed appearance does not mean physical
pixel aspect changed.

47
00:03:04,197 --> 00:03:08,137
Adding blocks changes the building itself.

48
00:03:08,617 --> 00:03:13,097
Distinguish construction from changes of
viewpoint.

49
00:03:13,097 --> 00:03:19,790
Tracking the same roof shows parts coming
forward or being hidden;

50
00:03:20,250 --> 00:03:23,570
camera position and direction change how the
scene reads.

51
00:03:25,463 --> 00:03:29,243
These pixels alone do not establish the game’s

52
00:03:29,683 --> 00:03:32,763
projection matrix or numeric zoom method.

53
00:03:34,717 --> 00:03:41,137
Our original diagram can hold position,

54
00:03:41,437 --> 00:03:45,570
field of view and output rectangle fixed and
change one at a time.

55
00:03:45,870 --> 00:03:48,330
Keep the overlap of the building faces in mind
as we

56
00:03:48,650 --> 00:03:52,390
construct the camera’s potential viewing
volume.

57
00:03:53,350 --> 00:03:56,770
The screen is flat, but a perspective camera’s

58
00:03:57,010 --> 00:03:59,870
potential view is a volume. Begin with four
corner rays.

59
00:04:00,170 --> 00:04:05,490
Cut the pyramid by near and far planes to
obtain the view frustum.

60
00:04:05,810 --> 00:04:11,930
A point inside is only potentially visible: an
opaque object may still occlude it.

61
00:04:11,990 --> 00:04:15,410
Moving translates the volume,

62
00:04:15,630 --> 00:04:17,830
rotating changes its orientation and changing
FOV changes its opening.

63
00:04:17,930 --> 00:04:19,910
Moving the output rectangle rightward is
different:

64
00:04:19,990 --> 00:04:22,310
the same view may simply be placed elsewhere.

65
00:04:22,310 --> 00:04:25,110
We will classify inside and outside with the
six boundary planes,

66
00:04:25,470 --> 00:04:27,250
beyond the outline of a pyramid.

67
00:04:28,239 --> 00:04:31,693
A frustum has left,right,top,bottom,near and
far boundaries;

68
00:04:31,693 --> 00:04:35,773
each boundary is an infinite plane.

69
00:04:35,812 --> 00:04:42,213
Choose the inner half-space of each plane and
retain their intersection.

70
00:04:42,493 --> 00:04:46,573
Our example faces positive z with near1 and
far11;

71
00:04:46,573 --> 00:04:50,213
these are defined values, not measurements of
the game.

72
00:04:50,613 --> 00:04:53,173
Points at z0.5,z6 and z12 are before

73
00:04:53,173 --> 00:04:56,773
near,between the distance planes and beyond
far.

74
00:04:56,773 --> 00:05:03,233
Being at z6 is insufficient if x extends
beyond a side boundary at that depth.

75
00:05:03,513 --> 00:05:06,533
A triangle crossing a boundary may retain a
portion.

76
00:05:06,713 --> 00:05:09,593
One outside vertex does not justify deleting
the whole primitive.

77
00:05:09,913 --> 00:05:13,553
Near and far also affect depth precision;

78
00:05:14,133 --> 00:05:17,193
the actual depth format and projection
convention matter.

79
00:05:17,673 --> 00:05:20,553
The next lecture calculates depth and division
order;

80
00:05:20,933 --> 00:05:23,633
retain both distance boundaries and four
screen-side boundaries.

81
00:05:24,317 --> 00:05:29,057
Floors are added while a low view reveals the
building’s side.

82
00:05:29,297 --> 00:05:34,650
Observe empty screen space and the roof
boundary.

83
00:05:34,990 --> 00:05:38,930
An elevated view reveals roofs and terraces;

84
00:05:39,250 --> 00:05:44,070
entering the view and becoming unoccluded are
different events.

85
00:05:46,023 --> 00:05:50,623
Walls overlapping frontally separate in an
oblique view;

86
00:05:50,883 --> 00:05:54,963
the same spatial relationship gives a
different arrangement.

87
00:05:57,177 --> 00:05:59,737
Later framing becomes wider and closer again.

88
00:05:59,937 --> 00:06:03,637
Record apparent size separately from which
faces are visible.

89
00:06:05,770 --> 00:06:09,770
Leaving the image does not prove that the
building vanished;

90
00:06:09,970 --> 00:06:13,470
consider the viewing volume.

91
00:06:15,583 --> 00:06:20,243
The recording does not reveal numeric near or
far planes.

92
00:06:20,383 --> 00:06:23,423
Keep observed pixels separate from defined
math examples.

93
00:06:25,517 --> 00:06:29,317
Our next diagram holds camera position fixed
while changing FOV,

94
00:06:29,657 --> 00:06:33,457
isolating it from movement.

95
00:06:34,317 --> 00:06:39,197
In a top view, connect the camera to the left
and right boundaries;

96
00:06:39,377 --> 00:06:42,197
their whole angle is horizontal FOV.

97
00:06:42,457 --> 00:06:44,577
The angle from the center to either edge is
half the

98
00:06:44,837 --> 00:06:48,277
FOV:45 degrees for a90-degree horizontal view.

99
00:06:48,517 --> 00:06:54,597
At depth3, the half-width is3 times tan45
degrees, or3.

100
00:06:54,717 --> 00:07:00,317
The horizontal span at that depth runs from−3
to3, width6; at depth6 it becomes12.

101
00:07:00,677 --> 00:07:04,057
A smaller FOV captures less at the same depth;

102
00:07:04,317 --> 00:07:07,417
the same object occupies a larger fraction of
the image.

103
00:07:07,697 --> 00:07:09,997
Horizontal and vertical FOV are distinct; an
aspect ratio links them,

104
00:07:10,157 --> 00:07:14,297
but equal numeric angles are a separate
choice.

105
00:07:15,037 --> 00:07:21,157
The source defines zoom relative to90-degree
FOV as one divided by tan of the half-angle.

106
00:07:21,537 --> 00:07:26,897
For90 degrees, tan45 is1, giving zoom1 as our
reference.

107
00:07:27,117 --> 00:07:29,857
Zoom2 requires half-angle tangent0.5;

108
00:07:30,077 --> 00:07:34,817
FOV is twice arctan of one half.

109
00:07:35,277 --> 00:07:42,237
The result is about53.13 degrees, not45: FOV
and zoom are not linearly proportional.

110
00:07:42,237 --> 00:07:45,157
With camera and objects fixed,

111
00:07:45,717 --> 00:07:47,857
central image sizes double because the
captured range narrows.

112
00:07:48,557 --> 00:07:51,617
Check whether tangent functions accept
radians;

113
00:07:51,897 --> 00:07:54,617
passing degree values directly changes the
result.

114
00:07:55,397 --> 00:07:57,537
This zoom is a projection parameter;

115
00:07:58,197 --> 00:08:01,217
orthographic zoom has a different meaning and
units.

116
00:08:01,850 --> 00:08:05,310
The players cross the candy town from
different positions.

117
00:08:05,430 --> 00:08:12,990
Compare character size with the surrounding
paths in view.

118
00:08:13,270 --> 00:08:17,570
A larger character does not prove a different
FOV:

119
00:08:17,870 --> 00:08:25,930
distance,direction and scene arrangement may
also differ.

120
00:08:26,230 --> 00:08:28,650
The gameplay supplies an observation question;

121
00:08:29,110 --> 00:08:33,910
controlled examples isolate position and
angle.

122
00:08:34,850 --> 00:08:39,330
For square pixels in a16:9 window,

123
00:08:39,330 --> 00:08:42,710
horizontal zoom1 requires vertical zoom16/9 to
preserve shape.

124
00:08:43,030 --> 00:08:48,470
Horizontal FOV is90 degrees;

125
00:08:48,470 --> 00:08:52,690
vertical FOV is twice arctan(9/16), about58.72
degrees.

126
00:08:52,890 --> 00:08:55,550
A wider window captures a larger horizontal
angle;

127
00:08:55,810 --> 00:08:59,350
using90 degrees on both axes can stretch a
circle horizontally.

128
00:08:59,470 --> 00:09:02,950
Vertical-to-horizontal zoom equals the
window’s physical aspect;

129
00:09:02,950 --> 00:09:06,410
nonsquare pixels need more than pixel counts.

130
00:09:06,830 --> 00:09:09,450
Include any physical display stretch in
addition to1920-by1080 counts;

131
00:09:09,850 --> 00:09:11,890
this connects the pixel-aspect calculation.

132
00:09:12,210 --> 00:09:14,930
If an engine exposes one FOV parameter,

133
00:09:15,230 --> 00:09:18,050
check whether it is horizontal or vertical and
how aspect derives the other.

134
00:09:18,270 --> 00:09:22,130
Resizing may preserve horizontal or vertical
FOV;

135
00:09:22,450 --> 00:09:24,350
verify what becomes visible or disappears.

136
00:09:25,133 --> 00:09:30,873
Track one roof while the waterside structure
grows,

137
00:09:31,093 --> 00:09:33,833
separating newly constructed faces.

138
00:09:35,933 --> 00:09:39,713
Wider framing changes to a closer view: the
roof

139
00:09:40,053 --> 00:09:43,773
grows while less surrounding water fits in the
image.

140
00:09:45,813 --> 00:09:49,533
Compare roofs at other depths as

141
00:09:49,533 --> 00:09:54,373
well as the foreground target.

142
00:09:56,193 --> 00:09:59,053
We will not label this game’s input as zoom or
movement;

143
00:09:59,053 --> 00:10:03,513
a defined two-depth example will calculate the
distinction.

144
00:10:04,133 --> 00:10:07,673
Place equal-sized panels at forward depths4
and8;

145
00:10:07,793 --> 00:10:11,633
perspective size is proportional to object
size and inversely proportional to depth.

146
00:10:11,913 --> 00:10:15,653
Doubling zoom without moving the camera
doubles both

147
00:10:16,033 --> 00:10:17,613
panels while preserving their relative
image-size ratio.

148
00:10:17,813 --> 00:10:24,573
Move the camera forward2 at fixed zoom: depths
change4→2 and8→6.

149
00:10:24,733 --> 00:10:28,853
The near panel grows by4/2=2; the far grows
by8/6=4/3,

150
00:10:28,853 --> 00:10:31,813
changing their relative image sizes.

151
00:10:31,813 --> 00:10:34,973
Both methods can double the front panel, but
the rear differs;

152
00:10:35,653 --> 00:10:37,793
camera movement and lens zoom are different.

153
00:10:38,193 --> 00:10:44,113
The denominator is forward-axis z, not the
Euclidean distance to an off-axis point.

154
00:10:44,513 --> 00:10:50,493
Use landmarks at multiple depths; one target
can hide changes in their relationship.

155
00:10:51,467 --> 00:10:56,967
Physical-camera FOV depends on sensor size as
well as focal length; consider sensor width.

156
00:10:57,187 --> 00:11:03,427
Horizontal FOV is twice arctan of sensor width
divided

157
00:11:03,427 --> 00:11:07,587
by twice focal length, using consistent units.

158
00:11:08,327 --> 00:11:15,707
A36mm-wide sensor with18mm focal length gives
ratio1 and horizontal FOV90 degrees.

159
00:11:15,907 --> 00:11:24,687
Keeping the sensor and using36mm focal length
gives ratio0.5 and FOV about53.13 degrees.

160
00:11:24,687 --> 00:11:30,347
An18mm sensor width at18mm focal length gives
the same FOV;

161
00:11:30,847 --> 00:11:32,847
one number alone is insufficient.

162
00:11:32,847 --> 00:11:37,387
Distinguish an arbitrary mathematical
projection-plane

163
00:11:37,787 --> 00:11:40,827
distance from the physical lens focal length.

164
00:11:41,227 --> 00:11:45,447
Check sensor,focal length and fit policy in
physical-camera settings.

165
00:11:45,847 --> 00:11:49,587
These calculations address FOV, not depth of
field or lens distortion.

166
00:11:50,233 --> 00:11:54,233
Use a large wall and rear roof as landmarks,

167
00:11:56,274 --> 00:12:00,834
separating newly added geometry from
orbit-revealed faces.

168
00:12:02,915 --> 00:12:06,515
Close framing emphasizes the wall;

169
00:12:08,375 --> 00:12:12,255
wide framing includes water and other
structures.

170
00:12:14,296 --> 00:12:16,516
Changing orientation affects visible area
beyond a

171
00:12:16,516 --> 00:12:24,457
simple enlargement factor; record viewpoint
conditions.

172
00:12:26,578 --> 00:12:31,396
The next interval adds blocks around an upper
terrace;

173
00:12:31,396 --> 00:12:37,096
distinguish existing railings from new walls.

174
00:12:39,535 --> 00:12:42,595
Wider framing returns to a close view;

175
00:12:44,553 --> 00:12:48,953
track front and rear landmarks together.

176
00:12:51,111 --> 00:12:54,731
We do not infer game camera calls;

177
00:12:54,991 --> 00:13:02,410
our factors2 and4/3 belong to the defined
diagram, not this recording.

178
00:13:04,508 --> 00:13:12,650
Gameplay motivates a readable result; the
mathematical example isolates

179
00:13:14,911 --> 00:13:17,731
its changed variables. Connect them while
respecting the evidence.

180
00:13:20,152 --> 00:13:23,612
If distance-based shrinking is unwanted,

181
00:13:25,893 --> 00:13:29,693
maps and modeling drawings can use another
projection.

182
00:13:30,433 --> 00:13:35,653
Perspective rays meet at the camera;
orthographic projection lines are parallel.

183
00:13:35,653 --> 00:13:38,993
Equal panels at depths2 and10 shrink
differently in

184
00:13:38,993 --> 00:13:42,913
perspective but have equal orthographic image
sizes.

185
00:13:42,913 --> 00:13:45,093
Orthographic projection does not preserve all
shape: rotating a panel

186
00:13:45,093 --> 00:13:49,433
reduces its projected width and collapses
viewing-direction information.

187
00:13:49,433 --> 00:13:53,213
Moving along the viewing direction leaves
surviving image sizes unchanged;

188
00:13:53,213 --> 00:13:57,073
clipping and occlusion still require checks.

189
00:13:57,073 --> 00:14:02,273
Its viewing volume is a box, described by
width and height rather than an opening angle.

190
00:14:02,493 --> 00:14:04,773
A constant scale is useful for maps and
drawings. This proof uses our diagram;

191
00:14:04,973 --> 00:14:09,293
Townscaper’s actual projection remains
unverified.

192
00:14:09,917 --> 00:14:14,517
Use an orthographic box8 by4.5 with

193
00:14:14,777 --> 00:14:18,177
aspect16:9 and a1920-by1080 output.

194
00:14:18,437 --> 00:14:20,657
Mapping width8 to1920 pixels gives240 pixels
per world unit;

195
00:14:20,917 --> 00:14:26,217
check the same scale vertically.

196
00:14:26,417 --> 00:14:30,897
Halve both dimensions to4 by2.25 for480 pixels
per unit,

197
00:14:30,997 --> 00:14:33,257
doubling image size without moving forward.

198
00:14:33,477 --> 00:14:39,077
The source’s orthographic zoom is2 divided by
size:0.25 for width8 and0.5 for width4.

199
00:14:39,077 --> 00:14:41,757
Perspective zoom comes from an angle ratio;

200
00:14:41,757 --> 00:14:47,237
orthographic zoom corresponds to inverse world
length.

201
00:14:47,457 --> 00:14:50,377
Changing only width creates unequal scales and
turns a circle into an ellipse;

202
00:14:50,637 --> 00:14:52,497
preserve physical output aspect.

203
00:14:52,777 --> 00:14:55,777
Orthographic rendering still uses near,far and
depth comparison;

204
00:14:55,957 --> 00:14:58,357
absence of shrinking does not remove depth.

205
00:14:59,167 --> 00:15:04,267
More construction and viewpoint changes reveal
terrace walls and roofs.

206
00:15:04,567 --> 00:15:09,300
Separate frontal and overhead views.

207
00:15:09,560 --> 00:15:13,740
Reading a different face requires another
direction;

208
00:15:14,120 --> 00:15:19,973
enlargement alone cannot reveal every hidden
roof or wall.

209
00:15:20,053 --> 00:15:23,493
The orthographic diagram demonstrated a
constant scale as an option,

210
00:15:23,853 --> 00:15:31,067
not an inferred property of the game.

211
00:15:31,067 --> 00:15:36,707
Choose viewing direction,perspective and range
before dividing the output;

212
00:15:37,107 --> 00:15:40,327
our next problem returns to two player
windows.

213
00:15:41,167 --> 00:15:45,387
Start with square pixels,1920

214
00:15:45,387 --> 00:15:49,807
by1080,horizontal FOV90,zoomX1 and zoomY16/9.

215
00:15:50,007 --> 00:15:55,427
Each vertical half is960 by1080 with aspect8:9
rather than16:9.

216
00:15:55,627 --> 00:16:01,667
Preserve vertical FOV and zoomY; their zoom
ratio must become8/9.

217
00:16:01,927 --> 00:16:05,467
This gives zoomX2 and horizontal FOV
about53.13 degrees,

218
00:16:05,467 --> 00:16:08,127
narrowing the visible sides.

219
00:16:08,367 --> 00:16:11,007
Preserving horizontal FOV instead is another
choice;

220
00:16:11,167 --> 00:16:14,587
declare the preserved axis before validating
shape and range.

221
00:16:14,867 --> 00:16:19,967
For the orthographic exercise,8 by4.5 gives240

222
00:16:20,187 --> 00:16:22,867
pixels per unit;4 by2.25 doubles it in the
same output.

223
00:16:23,207 --> 00:16:26,227
Neither exercise moved the camera forward;

224
00:16:26,227 --> 00:16:29,307
name output-aspect and projection changes
separately in code.

225
00:16:29,933 --> 00:16:37,273
The final example changes from a close roof
and frontal view to wider,elevated framing.

226
00:16:40,747 --> 00:16:45,347
Later blocks change the building itself.

227
00:16:45,347 --> 00:16:48,747
New faces differ from existing faces revealed
by another view.

228
00:16:52,160 --> 00:16:55,740
Write what to observe,from which
direction,over

229
00:16:55,740 --> 00:17:02,353
what range and in which output window.

230
00:17:02,753 --> 00:17:05,973
Change one condition and compare the same
landmarks

231
00:17:05,973 --> 00:17:11,253
to check both readability and the camera
calculation.

232
00:17:11,933 --> 00:17:16,013
We separated what a camera sees from where the
result goes:

233
